    "passage": "The small hill station looked especially beautiful after the rain. Drops of water still clung to the pine needles, and a thin mist floated slowly across the valley. Binya walked carefully along the narrow mountain path with a basket of apricots balanced on her head. The earth smelled fresh and clean, and from somewhere in the forest came the soft call of a cuckoo. At the edge of the road stood an old cherry tree. Beneath it sat Grandfather Hari, wrapped in a faded woollen shawl, watching the clouds drift over the hills. Every morning, he came to the same spot with his walking stick and a small tin cup of tea.\n\n“Good morning, Grandfather,” Binya called cheerfully. “Ah, Binya,” he replied with a smile. “You are late today.” “The rain flooded the lower path,” she explained. “I had to walk through the forest trail.”\n\nGrandfather Hari nodded slowly. “The forest keeps many secrets after rain,” he said. “When I was young, I once lost my way there for two whole days.” Binya laughed. “You must have been frightened!” “Not frightened,” he said thoughtfully. “Only lonely. But the trees became my friends. In the hills, one is never truly alone.” A cool wind rustled through the branches above them. For a few moments neither spoke. They listened to the distant sound of cowbells and the steady murmur of the river far below.\n\nThen Grandfather Hari pointed toward a small green shoot growing beside the cherry tree. “That little plant was only a seed when your father was a boy,” he said proudly. “Now look at it. Someday it will give shade to travellers long after we are gone.” Binya looked at the young tree quietly. Somehow, the hills suddenly seemed older and wiser than before.",
