    "question": "How many triangles are there in the given figure?\nA staircase of three right triangles above a large triangle. Three vertical lines V1 (leftmost), V2 (middle) and V3 (rightmost) all run down to a common horizontal base line; V1 is the tallest, V2 shorter, V3 shortest, their tops stepping down and to the right. From the top of each vertical a hypotenuse runs down-left at roughly 45 degrees. Triangle 1: from the top of V1 the hypotenuse falls to the left end of a short horizontal H1, which runs right to meet V1. Triangle 2: from the top of V2 the hypotenuse falls to the left end of horizontal H2, which runs right to V2; this hypotenuse passes exactly through the point where H1 meets V1. Triangle 3: from the top of V3 a long diagonal falls down-left, crossing V2 at the right-hand end of H2 and crossing V1 at the left-hand end of horizontal H3 (H3 runs right from V1 to V3), and continues all the way down to the left-hand end of the base line. Finally, from the right-hand end of the base line a slanted side rises up-left to the point where H3 meets the top of V3, closing the large bottom triangle on its right. The outer left and right slants do not meet each other, so no single all-enclosing triangle exists.",
