    "difficulty": "medium",
    "question": "Read the passage carefully and answer the questions that follow : It is common knowledge that there has been wide deforestation in recent years. According to conservative estimates, five million acres have been denuded of forests. This has brought about widespread climatic change all over the country – leading to recurring drought and other natural calamities. Erosion of topsoil in the catchment areas has resulted in the tragedy of yearly flooding of our major rivers. And flood control measures have not been effective because those have been limited to superficial remedies like building embankments rather than rectifying the root cause by starting a vigorous afforestation drive. Deforestation of our hills, in particular, has led to soil erosion and landslides on unprecedented scale, threatening the survival of some of our popular hill stations. The population increase has not spared the hills. In the towns more and more areas are being cleared to make room for houses and roads are being built in the fond hope of carrying the fruits of development to the hinterland. This is inevitable, of course, but it need not have been indiscriminately, without a thought to the negative consequences. As things are, the natural drainage of these areas has been upset and water seeping into bare earth has led to slipping and sinking of land. Piped water which is being introduced in hills townships without developing adequate drainage facilities has also added to the threat of rapid erosion. QUESTIONS :\na. Describe the extent of deforestation in India.(3)\nb. What has been the result of deforestation?(3)\nc. Why has the flood control measures failed in our country?(3)\nd. How does population increase affect the hills?(3) (i)to make bare(ii)happen again and again (iii)serious misfortunes(iv)inner part of a country (v)set right",
    "explanation": "",
    "sourceReview": true,
    "sourceNote": "Written response and transcription awaiting source review.",
    "source": "Inspector of Factories under Labour Employment Department · 2019",
    "year": 2019,
    "sourceHref": "/papers/group-b/iof-2019-english.pdf#page=2"
